          },
          "text": "The oil spill in the Gulf of #Mexico continues tp spread. The spill has already affected more than 630 kilometers of coastline, and 51 sites with tar have been identified. Non-governmental organizations warn that the impacts on ecosystems and communities are becoming increasingly severe.\n\nReefs at risk- The status of the 125 coral and rocky reefs that make up the Gulf of Mexico Reef Corridor remains unknown. These ecosystems are vital for biodiversity and the livelihoods of around 16,000 fishing families, who depend directly on them for survival.\n\nMexican authorities downplay the emergency-  Veracruz Governor Rocío Nahle has downplayed the situation, claiming that only “drops” of oil have reached the beaches and that they “are clean.” However, reports contradict this account.\n\nWildlife affected- The disaster has already claimed victims: at least 7 sea turtles, 2 dolphins, 2 manatees, and 1 pelican have been found contaminated with oil, most of them dead.\n\nMangroves and protected species in danger- The mangroves of Laguna del Ostión—red, black, and white mangroves—have also been damaged. This unique ecosystem is home to the hairy crab and the blue crab, species protected under NOM-059, as well as migratory birds and otters in the tributary rivers. (Source: Voices In Movement)"
